method-class.ts:378","src/daemon/threads/workers.ts:40","src/daemon/threads/workers.ts:73","src/workflow/code-review/task-review/coordinator.ts:83","src/workflow/code-review/task-review/coordinator.ts:143","src/shared/process-group.ts:76","src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts:94","src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:1141"],"limitations":[],"advice":[]},{"chunkId":"runtime","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The revised design adequately specifies per-review ownership, trusted routing, host-owned native process leases and bounded bootstrap supervision. Full-daemon crash recovery remains underspecified: the required resource fence has no defined reconstruction or safe clearance protocol.","findings":[{"id":"runtime-003","severity":"MED","location":"/body/dataModelChanges/5; LLD.md:161","message":"Define how resource fences survive and are cleared after a full daemon crash. The registry is explicitly process-lifetime state, command leases live in the main daemon, and the proposed checkpoint additions contain no process ownership or disposal record. If the daemon dies while a detached CLI is running, its handles, lease inventory and disposal observers disappear together. The requirement to retain a targeted fence until disposal is established therefore lacks a restart discovery rule and an accepted clearance mechanism. Recovery must otherwise either remain blocked indefinitely or risk dispatching alongside an orphaned command. Specify conservative fence reconstruction from durable state, the evidence/authority required to clear it, and a real daemon-crash regression proving replacement remains blocked while an old child survives and unrelated reviews remain usable.","evidenceReferences":["/body/dataModelChanges/0 — registry entries are process-lifetime routing facts","/body/dataModelChanges/1 — proposed durable admission fields","docs/standalone/isolate-each-code-design-review-its-E20261109e4af6312/S001/LLD.md:157","docs/standalone/isolate-each-code-design-review-its-E